# AST play — a Spw parse read literally as the plan of a game
# A design, proposed. Built as book/scripts/modules/spw-play-graph.mjs; held by npm run play-graph:check. Structures and counts only: nothing here has been played.
#>lore_land_surfaces_ast_play
#:surface #!proposal
#:operation #!prime
#:fixity #!experimental
#:layer #!pragmatics

~<rationale>{
  Every Spw text is already shaped like a level. Operations are places with rules, containers are kinds of space, sequences are paths, references are doors. Read literally, the tree a parser returns is a map, and the same map can be played as several kinds of game. That makes the theory playable from the other side: a reader who writes Spw is also drawing a level, and a level drawn by playing is a sentence that can be read. The lane runner this site already plays is one projection of many.
}

^"mapping"{
  operation: "a place, whose rule is its sigil: ! an event that fires on arrival, ? a probe that reveals, ~ something left open, @ a point of view, & a junction, * a thing to collect, ^ a key that binds upward and opens the area above, # a sign, . solid ground, = a lean, % a gauge, $ what it is made of"
  frame: "[ ] an arena: a choice among its items"
  body: "{ } an area: gathers charge as it is entered, discharges on the way out"
  scope: "( ) a turn: its items taken together, as one combo"
  capsule: "< > a passage: a concept gate; @mind <concept>{ space } couples a mind on the left to a space on the right"
  path_ref: "a door to another surface"
  reference: "@word a view: whose eyes the next place is seen through"
  binding: "key: value a sign posted in the place"
  leaf: "a literal, identifier or lone valence word is an item lying there; a particle (#>, #:, #!) is a mark on the map"
  sequence: "a path in order; the steps of one sequence are siblings, and siblings are branches"
  whole: "a text that does not read whole maps to nothing and says so: a level built from half a parse would be built from guesses"
}

^"projections"{
  lane_runner: "the top-level path is the road; each step a room passing, named by its sigil; what stands inside comes down the lanes"
  deck_builder: "every place a card: sigil the suit, depth the cost, what it holds the power"
  metroidvania: "every body a region behind the sigil of the place that owns it, which must be found somewhere else first; a region whose key is found nowhere in the text stays sealed until another surface brings it"
  rhythm_track: "each top-level step a beat; ! on the downbeat, * and ^ lift, the sigils with no phase are ghost notes; each frame a bar as long as its choices"
  roguelike_floor: "every frame a fork chosen by a seed; the same seed walks the same floor; how many floors a text can make is the product of its frames"
  garden: "every body a bed that grows with what stands in it and is harvested at its closing brace"
  abstraction_ladder: "areas climb from literal rooms { } to chosen arenas [ ] to pattern zones ( ) to concept gates < >; a gate becomes a place only when a mind enters, and the mind coupled to it (or the nearest @ before it) decides what kind of place (gradients.spw)"
}

^"levels_from_this_tree"{
  pie_rules: "1208 nodes, 371 places, 96 areas, 266 arenas; 16 rooms on the road with 828 things; 371 cards in 11 suits; all 96 regions open in 4 rounds; about 10 to the 12 floors; one concept gate"
  chapter_01: "305 nodes, depth 15; 12 rooms, 226 things; 40 cards in 10 suits; 19 of 26 regions open in 2 rounds, 7 sealed; 16016 floors; ladder 26 literal, 28 instance, 1 pattern, 11 concept"
  chapter_14: "303 nodes, depth 15; 12 rooms, 220 things; 42 cards in 12 suits; 15 of 25 regions open, 10 sealed; 26208 floors; 16 concept gates"
  liminal_physics: "170 nodes; 14 rooms; 2 of 3 regions open, 1 sealed; 12 floors"
  board_line: "12 nodes; one region, sealed: the line holds the only ^ it has, so its key must come from another surface"
  way_line: "10 nodes; one region, sealed, for the same reason"
  board_and_way: "read together, each line holds the key the other needs, and both regions open in one round: two set-ups that unlock each other"
  mind_and_concept: "@boof <boon>{ {bone} } becomes a concept gate boon, implemented by the mind boof as a literal room"
}

^"links"{
  formulas: "book/scripts/modules/spw-formulas.mjs and .spw/surfaces/formulas.spw, the castable side: formulas read, said and computed at the desk"
  gradients: "@spw/surfaces/gradients.spw, the reasons for a sigil by scale, span and abstraction"
  physics: "@spw/surfaces/liminal-physics.spw, the sigils as the physics of the page"
}

!guards[
  "The mapping reads a sigil only in its own role. A projection that needs a sigil to mean something new is the wrong projection"
  "A projection is structure and counts until someone plays it. No claim that a level is fun rests on its numbers"
  "Chapter mirrors are read as maps of the book, never rewritten to make better levels"
]

?open[
  "Which projection is worth a playable prototype first: the abstraction ladder is the most Spw, the deck builder the most familiar"
  "Should a sealed region be opened by a door to another surface (a ~ path), making surfaces into a world map"
  "Could a reader draw a level by writing a line at the desk, and play it in the lanes"
  "Do chapter mirrors keep their rooms as rooms when played as a metroidvania, or does the book want a different map"
]
